---
/**
 * website/src/components/CampaignCalls.astro — the calls to action (go-to-market plan, Task
 * G2.2; spec §6, section 5; reconciliation F17, F22, D401).
 *
 * **Two links and no form.** The spec's calls to action are a booking, an install and a chat,
 * and this section renders all three: **Book a demo** as a plain `<a href>` to the Calendly
 * link `website/data/landing.yaml` writes, **Install free** as a link to the repository
 * `website/data/site.yaml` names, and **Chat with the founder** as the `wa.me` link the loader
 * derives from the file's WhatsApp number and the variant's own pre-fill message. The page asks
 * a visitor for nothing — there is no waitlist and no email field anywhere on a campaign page —
 * which is why the plain link and not an embed is the booking's first form here.
 *
 * **Why a plain link and not the embed.** G2.3 renders the Calendly embed inline behind the
 * marketing consent (D401), and G2.4 owns the two events these links fire. Both need this link
 * to already exist: a visitor who declines the marketing consent keeps a working way to book,
 * and the plain link is the thing the consent gate is measured against. So this component names
 * no vendor asset, carries no `iframe`, and passes no query of its own — the UTM parameters
 * G2.4 passes through go into the embed's address and onto neither of these links (F17, F22).
 *
 * **The links are the files', not this component's.** The booking URL and the chat URL are read
 * from `website/data/landing.yaml` and the repository from `website/data/site.yaml`, both passed
 * in by the page: a link typed here would be a second copy of a fact, and the copy that drifts.
 *
 * **The band is a field of the accent** (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-30, finding 4). The
 * closing call to action used to be three outlined buttons on the ground, and the page ended in
 * the same colour it began in. It is now the page's one full-width field of flat colour: the
 * band takes the accent, its words and its two quiet actions are set in the ground, and the
 * booking is the one filled control on it. Nothing on the field carries a hairline border — the
 * two quiet actions are text at a thumb's target size rather than boxes — so the band reads as
 * one colour with three actions on it rather than as three controls with a background. **Its
 * corners are rounded generously** (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-30, D412's finding 2): the field
 * was a hard-cornered band and it is cut at `--radius-xl`, the site's largest radius step and
 * the one the steps' panel above it carries, so the page's two fields are one shape. It is
 * still flat colour — no gradient, no shadow at rest — and the radius is the whole of the
 * change to its box.
 *
 * **The field is why the focus ring is named here.** The site's ring is the accent
 * (`base.css`'s `:focus-visible`), and on a field of the accent a ring in it is a ring nobody
 * can see, so this band paints its own ring in the ground — the same colour its words are in,
 * and the pair `website/test/a11y.mjs` computes for the filled control.
 *
 * **Its parts rise into place once.** The band is a `data-reveal-block`, so `site.js`'s own
 * entrance holds back the heading, the sentence and the three actions while they are below the
 * first screen and shows them in reading order as the band enters — with no script nothing is
 * ever held back, and a reader who asked their system for less motion is served the band still
 * (`public/scripts/site.js`). The travel is the 24 px the owner asked the campaign's sections
 * for rather than the home page's 12 px, declared inside the same `no-preference` query the
 * rest of the campaign's motion is.
 *
 * **The two ids are the events' hook** (G2.4): `site.js` knows the two links by the ids the
 * markup gives them, and presses them into GA4's `install_click` and `whatsapp_click` once the
 * visitor has granted analytics. They are the only thing about this section a script reads.
 *
 * **Each action carries its own small mark** (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-30, D412's finding 1).
 * Three rows of type on a field of colour read as three lines of a paragraph rather than as
 * three things a reader can do, so the booking has a calendar beside it, the install a
 * download and the chat a bubble — inline SVG from `CampaignIcon.astro`, `aria-hidden` because
 * the link's own words are the meaning, and in the control's own colour so the band paints no
 * second one. The plate lifts 2 px on hover and each mark nudges 2 px the way its own action
 * goes — the calendar up, the arrow down into its tray, the bubble out — which is the "small
 * lift or icon nudge" the owner asked for, and it is 2 px of `transform` on two elements
 * rather than a second colour. **The movement is inside the `no-preference` query with the
 * band's other motion**, so a reader who asked their system for less is served the same band
 * still: the hover's colour and underline are what answers them.
 */
import CampaignIcon from './CampaignIcon.astro'

interface Props {
  /** The owner's booking link, from `website/data/landing.yaml`. */
  calendlyURL: string
  /** The repository, from `website/data/site.yaml`. */
  repository: string
  /** The variant's `wa.me` link, from `website/data/landing.yaml`. */
  whatsappURL: string
}

const { calendlyURL, repository, whatsappURL } = Astro.props
---

<section class="campaign-calls" data-section="calls-to-action" data-reveal-block>
  <h2 class="campaign-calls-title display-lg">See it run on your own repository</h2>
  <p class="campaign-calls-line">Book half an hour, bring a repository, and watch a task become a pull request under your own caps.</p>
  <ul class="campaign-calls-list">
    <li>
      <a class="campaign-calls-primary" href={calendlyURL}><CampaignIcon name="calendar" />Book a demo</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a class="campaign-calls-secondary" id="campaign-install" href={repository} rel="noopener"><CampaignIcon name="download" />Install free</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a class="campaign-calls-secondary" id="campaign-whatsapp" href={whatsappURL} rel="noopener"><CampaignIcon name="chat" />Chat with the founder</a>
    </li>
  </ul>
  {/* The booking widget's place, empty and hidden until the visitor grants the marketing
      consent: `site.js` writes the booking address onto this element and unhides it, and
      Calendly's script — fetched after that grant and never before — fills it. It carries the
      vendor's own class because that class is how its script finds the container, and it is
      drawn before the grant as nothing at all: the plain link above is the booking's first form,
      and a visitor who declines never sees a frame (D401, F17). */}
  <div class="campaign-calls-embed calendly-inline-widget" id="calendly-embed" hidden></div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Tokens only (W1.2). The three actions stack on a phone — the booking first, at the width of
     a thumb — and sit in a row from the wide breakpoint, which is the layout the static half of
     `website/test/landing-pages.mjs` reads; the rendered reading at both widths is the owner's
     check (G2.5). */
  .campaign-calls {
    padding-block: var(--space-xl) var(--space-3xl);
    padding-inline: var(--frame-gutter);
    border-radius: var(--radius-xl);
    background: var(--accent);
    color: var(--ground);
  }

  /* The band's words are the ground's, on the accent: the same pair the filled control below is
     drawn in, and the one `website/test/a11y.mjs` computes at the body ratio. `.display-lg` sets
     the site's own text colour, so the title names this band's. */
  .campaign-calls-title {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--ground);
    font-size: clamp(var(--font-size-lg), 4.4vw, var(--font-size-3xl));
  }

  /* The sentence under it is the body's size on a phone and the body's larger step where the
     viewport has room, the same pair the steps section sets: it is body copy under a heading,
     and it stays under the heading at every width. */
  .campaign-calls-line {
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--ground);
    font-size: clamp(var(--font-size-base), 1.9vw, var(--font-size-lg));
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  .campaign-calls-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    margin: var(--space-2xl) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .campaign-calls-primary,
  .campaign-calls-secondary {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* The mark and the label are one row with the site's own step between them, so the two are
       spaced by the layout rather than by a margin on the icon. */
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    box-sizing: border-box;
    min-height: 56px;
    padding: 0 var(--space-xl);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    font-weight: var(--weight-black);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* The booking, and the band's one plate: the ground as its fill and the page's own text as its
     label, which is the inverse of the hero's filled control and the reason it reads as the
     action the band is for. */
  .campaign-calls-primary {
    background: var(--ground);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* The other two: the field's own ink, no plate and no rule, so the band is one colour with
     three actions on it rather than three boxes. They keep the control's own target size. */
  .campaign-calls-secondary {
    color: var(--ground);
  }

  .campaign-calls-primary:active,
  .campaign-calls-secondary:active {
    transform: scale(0.97);
  }

  /* The ring, in the band's own ink: the site's ring is the accent, and an accent ring on the
     accent is a ring a reader cannot see (base.css's `:focus-visible`). The `outline-color` is
     named rather than the shorthand so the site's width and offset stand. */
  .campaign-calls-primary:focus-visible,
  .campaign-calls-secondary:focus-visible {
    outline-color: var(--ground);
  }

  /* The booking widget's frame, once the visitor's grant has put it in the page. Calendly's
     widget needs a height it can lay a month of days out in — its own snippet writes 700px — and
     without one the frame collapses to nothing and a consenting visitor sees the two buttons and
     a blank strip. The height is on the class rather than inline on the element, and the
     element's `hidden` still wins while it is hidden because nothing here sets `display`. */
  .campaign-calls-embed {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
    min-height: 700px;
  }

  .campaign-calls-embed[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    /* The phone's own block: the band's closing air is a step smaller, so the last thing on the
       page is the three actions rather than a screen of empty colour under them. */
    .campaign-calls {
      padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-2xl);
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .campaign-calls {
      padding-block: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-4xl);
    }

    .campaign-calls-list {
      flex-direction: row;
      align-items: center;
      gap: var(--space-lg);
    }
  }

  /* The feedback on the controls, and the reader's answer to the motion. The hover is for a
     pointer that hovers and for no other kind (the site's own query); the quiet actions answer
     by underlining, which is the site's own idiom for a link, and the plate answers by going one
     tone step up. Both are declared only for a reader who has not asked their system for less
     motion, so nothing here is undone by a rule elsewhere. */
  @media screen and (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .campaign-calls-primary,
    .campaign-calls-secondary {
      transition:
        background-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out),
        color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out),
        transform var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out);
    }

    /* The mark's own travel, which is the second half of the controls' answer: the icon
       transitions with the plate it sits on, so the two move as one control rather than the
       icon arriving late. */
    .campaign-calls-primary :global(.campaign-icon),
    .campaign-calls-secondary :global(.campaign-icon) {
      transition: transform var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out);
    }

    /* The lift and the three nudges, for a pointer that hovers and for no other kind (the
       site's own query): the plate rises 2 px and the mark moves 2 px past it the way its own
       action goes. Both are `transform` and nothing else — the colour the control answers with
       is the rule above this block, which every reader gets.
       **`:not(:active)` is what keeps the press visible**: the plate's `:active` scale is
       declared above this block at the same specificity, so without the exclusion a mouse press
       — which is `:hover` and `:active` at once — would be answered by the lift and the press
       would read as nothing at all. A pressed control is not a hovered one. */
    @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
      .campaign-calls-primary:hover:not(:active),
      .campaign-calls-secondary:hover:not(:active) {
        transform: translateY(-2px);
      }

      /* One nudge each, so a mark's direction is its own action's rather than one direction the
         three share: the booking's calendar rises with its plate, the install's arrow carries on
         down into the tray it is drawn over, and the chat's bubble goes out. */
      .campaign-calls-primary:hover:not(:active) :global(.campaign-icon[data-icon='calendar']) {
        transform: translateY(-2px);
      }

      .campaign-calls-secondary:hover:not(:active) :global(.campaign-icon[data-icon='download']) {
        transform: translateY(2px);
      }

      .campaign-calls-secondary:hover:not(:active) :global(.campaign-icon[data-icon='chat']) {
        transform: translateX(2px);
      }
    }

    /* The campaign's own travel: the owner asked these four pages for 16–32 px of it, and the
       home page's 12 px is what `base.css` gives a held-back part. `site.js` sets no
       `data-reveal` under `reduce` at all; `screen` keeps a printed page where it is, since
       `base.css` shows every held-back part at `transform: none` for print. */
    .campaign-calls [data-reveal]:not([data-revealed]) {
      transform: translateY(24px);
    }
  }

  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .campaign-calls-primary:hover {
      background: var(--panel);
    }

    .campaign-calls-secondary:hover {
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
      text-underline-offset: 0.25em;
    }
  }
</style>
